1:\textbf{Motivation and Problem Statement.}  Solving multivariate
2:quadratic  polynomial systems is a fundamental
3:problem in Information Theory. Moreover, \emph{random}
4:instances seem difficult to solve. Consequently, the security of
5:several multivariate cryptosystems relies on its hardness,
6:either directly (e.g., HFE \citep{Pat96}, UOV
7:\citep{KipPatGou99},\ldots) or indirectly (e.g., McEliece~\citep{EuroCrypt10}).
8:In some cases, systems of special types have to be solved, but recent proposals like the new Polly Cracker type
9:cryptosystem \citep{Asia2011} rely on the hardness of solving \emph{random}
10:systems of equations. This motivates the study of the complexity
11:of generic polynomial systems. A particularly important case for applications in cryptology is the Boolean case; in that case both the coefficients
12:and the solutions of the system are over \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\). The main problem to be solved is the following: 
14:\begin{quote}
15:{\bf The Boolean Multivariate Quadratic Polynomial Problem (Boolean MQ)}\\
16:{\bf Input:} \((f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})\in\mathbb{F}_2[x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}]^m\) with \(\deg(f_{i})=2\) for $i=1,\dots,m$.\\
17:{\bf Question:} Find -- if any -- \emph{all} \(z\in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}\) such that \(f_{1}(z)=\cdots=f_{m}(z)=0\).
18:\end{quote}
19:Another related problem stems from the fact that in many cryptographic applications, 
20:it is sufficient to find \emph{at least one} solution of the corresponding polynomial system (in that case a solution is the original clear message or is related to the secret key). For instance, the stream cipher QUAD~\citep{QUADEuro,QUADJSC} relies on the iteration of a set of multivariate quadratic polynomials over  \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\)
21:so that the security of the keystream generation is related to the difficulty  of finding at least one solution of the Boolean MQ problem. Thus, we also consider the following variant of the
22:Boolean MQ\ problem:
23:\begin{quote}
24:{\bf The Boolean Multivariate Quadratic Polynomial Satisfiability
25:Problem (Boolean MQ
26:SAT)}\\
27:{\bf Input:} \((f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})\in\mathbb{F}_2[x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}]^m\) with \(\deg(f_{i})=2\) for $i=1,\dots,m$.\\
28:{\bf Question:} Find -- if any -- \emph{one} \(z\in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}\) such that \(f_{1}(z)=\cdots=f_{m}(z)=0\).
29:\end{quote}
30:Testing for the existence of a solution is an NP-complete problem (it is plainly in NP and 3-SAT can be reduced to it~\citep{FraenkelYesha1979}). 
31:Clearly, the Boolean MQ\ problem is at least as hard as Boolean MQ SAT, while an exponential complexity is achieved by exhaustive search. 
33:Throughout this paper, \emph{random} means distributed  according to the uniform distribution (given $m$ and $n$, a random quadratic polynomial is uniformly distributed if all its coefficients are independently and uniformly distributed over $\mathbb{F}_2$).
34:The relation between the difficulties of  Boolean MQ and Boolean MQ SAT depends on the relative values of~$m$ and~$n$.
35:When \(m> n\), the number of solutions of the algebraic system is 0 or 1 with large
36:probability and thus finding one or all solutions is very similar, while when~$m=n$, the probability that a random system has at least one solution
37:over \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\)
38:tends to \(1-\frac{1}{e}\approx 0.63\) for large~$n$~\citep{FusBac07}. 
39:Hence if we have to find a
40:least one solution of a system with \(m<n\) equations in \(n\)
41:variables it is enough to specialize \(n-m\) variables randomly
42:in \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\); the resulting system has at least one
43: solution with limit probability \(0.63\) and is easier to solve (since
44:the number of equations and variables is only \(m\)). Consequently,
45:in the remainder of this article we restrict ourselves to the
46:case \(m\geq n\).
49:To the best of our knowledge, in the \emph{worst case}, the best complexity bound to solve the Boolean MQ problem is obtained by a modified exhaustive search in $4\log_2(n)2^n$ operations~\citep{CHES10}.
50:Being able to decrease significantly this complexity is a long-standing
51:open problem and is the main goal of this article.  It is
52:crucial for practical applications to have sharp estimates of the
53:asymptotic complexity: it is especially important in the cryptographic
54:context where this value may have a strong impact on the sizes of the
55:keys needed to reach a given level of security.
58:\medskip
60:\noindent\textbf{Main results. }We describe a new algorithm \textsf{BooleanSolve} that solves Boolean MQ for determined or overdetermined systems ($m=\alpha n$ with $\alpha\ge1$). We show how to adapt it to solve the Boolean MQ SAT problem.
61:This algorithm has deterministic and Las Vegas variants, depending on the choice of some linear algebra subroutines. Our main result is:
62:\begin{thm} The Boolean MQ Problem is solved by Algorithm {\sf BooleanSolve}. If~$m=n$ and the system fulfills algebraic assumptions detailed in Theorem~\ref{thm:globalcompl}, then this algorithm uses a number of arithmetic operations in~${\mathbb F}_2$ that is:
63:\begin{itemize}
64:\item $O(2^{0.841n})$ using the deterministic variant;
65:\item of expectation $O( 2^{0.792 n})$ using the \emph{Las Vegas} probabilistic variant.
66:\end{itemize}
67:\end{thm}
68:Recall that for a probabilistic algorithm of the Las Vegas type, the result is always correct, but the complexity is a random variable. Here its expectation is controlled well. 
70:\noindent\textbf{Outline.}
71:Our algorithm is a variant of the hybrid
72:approach by \cite{BetFauPer09,BetFauPer12}: we specialize the last \(k\) variables to all possible values, and check the consistency of the specialized overdetermined systems~$(\tilde{f}_1,\dots,\tilde{f}_m)$ in the remaining variables~$x_1,\dots,x_\ell$.
74:This consistency check is done by searching for polynomials~$h_1,\dots,h_{m+\ell}$ in $x_1,\dots,x_\ell$ such that
75:\begin{equation}\label{consistency}
76:h_1\tilde{f}_1+\dots+h_m\tilde{f}_m+h_{m+1}x_1(1-x_1)+\dots+h_{m+\ell} x_\ell(1-x_\ell)=1.
77:\end{equation}
78:If such polynomials exist then obviously the system is not consistent. Given a bound~$d$ on the degrees of the polynomials~$h_i\tilde{f}_i$ and $h_{m+i}x_i(1-x_i)$, the existence of the $h_i$ can be checked by linear algebra. The corresponding matrix is known as the Macaulay matrix in degree \(d\). It 
79:is a matrix whose rows contain the coefficients of the
80:polynomials \(\tilde{f}_{i}\) and $x_i(1-x_i)$ multiplied by all monomials of degree
81:at most $d-2$, each column corresponding to a monomial of degree at most~$d$. Taking into account the special shape of the polynomials~$x_i(1-x_i)$ leads to a more compact variant that we call the boolean Macaulay matrix (see Section \ref{sec:algo}).
83:When linear algebra on the Macaulay matrix in degree~$d$ produces a solution of~\eqref{consistency}, the corresponding~$h_i$'s give a certificate of inconsistency.
84:Otherwise, our algorithm proceeds with an exhaustive search in the remaining variables.
85:In summary, our algorithm is a partial exhaustive search where the Macaulay matrices
86:permit to prune branches of the search tree. The correctness of the algorithm is clear.
88:The key point making the algorithm efficient is the choice of~\(k\) and $d$. If $d$ is large, then the cost of the linear algebra stage becomes high. If $d$ is small, the matrices are small, but many branches with no solutions are not pruned and require an exhaustive search. This is where we use the relation between the Macaulay matrix and Gr\"obner bases. We define a \emph{witness degree}
89:$\dwit$, which has the property that any polynomial in a minimal
90:Gr\"obner basis of the system 
91:is obtained as a linear
92:combination of the rows of the Macaulay matrix in degree \(\dwit \).
93:Hilbert's Nullstellensatz states that the system has no solution \emph{if and only if}
94:$1$ belongs to the ideal generated by the polynomials, which implies that~1 is a linear combination of the rows of the Macaulay matrix in degree \(\dwit\), making $\dwit$ an upper bound for the choice of~$d$ in~\eqref{consistency}.
96:Our complexity estimates rely on a good control of the witness degree. For a homogeneous polynomial ideal, the classical Hilbert function of the degree $d$ is the dimension of the vector space obtained as the quotient of the polynomials of degree~$d$ by the polynomials of degree~$d$ in the ideal.  
97:The witness degree is bounded by the first degree where the Hilbert function of the ideal generated by the homogenized equations is~0.
98:Under the algebraic assumption of boolean semi-regularity (see Definition \ref{def:bool semi
99:reg}), we obtain an explicit expression for the generating series of the Hilbert function, known as the Hilbert series of the ideal. From there, in Proposition \ref{prop:dregh}, using the saddle-point method as in~\citep{BarFauSal04,BarFauSal05,Bar04}, we
100:show 
101: that when  
102:   \(m= \alpha n\) and \(n\rightarrow\infty\), the witness degree behaves like \({\dwit}\le c_{\alpha}n\) for a constant \(c_\alpha\) that we determine explicitly.
103:Informally, boolean semi-regularity amounts to demanding a ``sufficient'' independence of the equations. In the case of infinite fields, a classical conjecture by \cite{Fro85} states that generic systems are semi-regular. In our context where the field is~$\mathbb{F}_2$, we give strong experimental evidence (Section \ref{sec:numBeta}) that for $n$ sufficiently large, boolean semi-regularity holds with probability very close to~1 for random systems.  
104:Thus, our complexity estimates for boolean semi-regular systems apply to a large class of systems in practice.
107:Once the witness degree is controlled, the size of the Macaulay matrix depends only on the choice of~$k$ and the optimal choice  depends on the complexity of the linear algebra stage.
108:In the Las Vegas version of Algorithm \textsf{BooleanSolve}, we exploit the sparsity of this matrix by using a variant of Wiedemann's algorithm~\citep{GieLobSau98} (following \cite{Wie86,KalSau91,Vil97}) for solving singular linear systems. 
109:In the deterministic version, we do not know of efficient ways to take advantage of the sparsity of the matrix, whence a slightly higher complexity bound.
110:We can then draw conclusions and obtain  a complexity estimate of the algorithm depending on $k/n$ and \(n\) (Proposition \ref{prop:complFirstStep}). The optimal value for \(k\) is \(\simeq 0.45\,n \) in the Las Vegas setting and \(\simeq 0.59\,n \) in the deterministic variant, completing the proof of our main theorem.
112:The complexity analysis is especially
113:important for practical applications in multivariate Cryptology based
114:on the Boolean MQ problem, since it shows that in order to reach a security of
115:$2^{s}$ (with $s$ large), one has to construct systems of boolean
116:quadratic equations with at least $s/0.7911\simeq 1.264 s$ variables.
118:\medskip
120:\noindent\textbf{Related works.}
121:Due to its practical importance, many algorithms have been designed to
122:solve the MQ problem in a wide range of contexts. First, generic
123:techniques for solving polynomial systems can be used. In particular,
124:Gr\"obner basis algorithms (such as Buchberger's algorithm
125:\citep{Buc65}, $F_4$ \citep{Fau99}, $F_5$ \citep{Fau02}, and FGLM
126:\citep{FauGiaLazMor93}) are well suited for this task. For instance,
127:the $F_5$ algorithm has broken several challenges of the HFE 
128:public-key cryptosystem \citep{FauJou03}. In the cryptanalysis
129:context, the XL algorithm~\citep{KipSha99} (which can be seen as a variant of Gr\"obner
130:basis algorithms \citep{ArsFauImaKawSug04}) has given rise to a large family of variants. All these
131:techniques are closely related to the Macaulay matrix,
132:introduced by \cite{Mac03} as a tool for elimination.
133:In order to reduce the cost of linear algebra for the efficient computation of the
134:resultant of multivariate polynomial systems, the idea of using
135:Wiedemann's algorithm on the Macaulay matrix has been proposed by \cite{CKY89}; however since the specificities of the Boolean
136:case are not taken into account, the complexity of applying \citep{CKY89}
137:to quadratic equations is \(O(2^{4\,n})\).
140:\cite{YanChe04} propose a heuristic analysis of the
141:FXL algorithm leading them to an upper bound $O(2^{0.875n})$ for the
142:complexity of solving the MQ problem over $\mathbb{F}_2$. In
143:particular, they give an explicit formula for the Hilbert series of
144:the ideal generated by the polynomials. However, 
145:the exact assumptions that
146:have to be verified by the input systems are unclear. Also, similar
147:results have been announced in \cite[Section 2.2]{CouYanChe04}, but
148:the analysis there relies on algorithmic assumptions (e.g., row
149:echelon form of sparse matrices in quadratic complexity) that are not
150:known to hold currently. Under these assumptions, the authors show that the
151:best trade-off between exhaustive search and row echelon form
152:computations in the FXL algorithm is obtained by specializing $0.45 n$ variables. This is the same value we obtain and prove with our algorithm. Also, a limiting behavior of the cost of the hybrid approach is obtained in~\cite{BetFauPer12} when the size of the finite field is big enough; these results are not applicable
153:over \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\).
155:Other algorithms have been proposed when the system has additional structural
156:properties. In particular, the Boolean MQ
157:problem also arises in satisfiability problems, since boolean
158:quadratic polynomials can be used for representing constraints. 
159:In these contexts, the systems are sparse and for such systems of higher degree the $2^n$ barrier has been broken~\citep{Sem08,Sem09}; similar results also exist for the $k$-SAT problem. Our algorithm does not exploit the extra structure induced by this type of sparsity and thus does not improve upon those results.
161:\medskip
162:\noindent\textbf{Organization of the article.} 
163:The main algorithm and the algebraic tools that are used throughout
164:the article are described in Section~\ref{sec:algo}. Then a complexity
165:analysis is performed in Section~\ref{sec:compl} by studying the
166:asymptotic behaviour of the witness degree and the sizes of the
167:Macaulay matrices involved, under algebraic assumptions. In
168:Section~\ref{sec:expmodel}, we provide a conjecture and strong
169:experimental evidence that these algebraic assumptions are verified
170:with probability close to $1$ for $n$ sufficiently large. Finally, in
171:Section \ref{sec:appli} we propose an extension of the main algorithm
172:that improves the quality of the linear filtering when $n$ is small. We
173:also show how the complexity results from Section \ref{sec:compl} can
174:be applied to the cryptosystem QUAD, leading to an evaluation of the sizes of the
175:parameters needed to reach a given level of security.
1:Let $m$ and $n$ be two positive integers and let
2:$R$ be the ring $\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n]$.  In the
3:following, the notation $\mathsf{Monomials}(d)$ stands for the set of monomials in
4:$R$ of degree at most $d$. 
6:Since we are looking for solutions of the system in $\mathbb{F}_2$
7:(and not in its algebraic closure), we have to take into account the relations
8:$x_i^2-x_i=0$. Therefore, we consider the application $\varphi$ 
9:mapping a monomial to its square-free part ($\varphi(\prod_{i=1}^n x_i^{a_i})=\prod_{i=1}^n x_i^{\min(a_i,1)}$) and extended to $R$ by
10:linearity.
12:If $(f_1,\ldots, f_m)\in\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n]^m$ is a system of polynomials, its homogenization is denoted by the system $(f_1^{(h)},\ldots,
13:f_m^{(h)})\in \mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n,h]$ and is
14:defined by
15:$$f_i^{(h)}(x_1,\ldots, x_n, h)=h^{\deg{(f_i)}} f_i\left(\frac{x_1}{h},\ldots, \frac{x_n}{h}\right).$$
17:In the sequel, we consider the classical \emph{grevlex} monomial ordering (\textbf{g}raded \textbf{rev}erse \textbf{lex}icographical), as defined for instance in \cite[\S2.2, Defn. 6]{CoxLitShe97}.
18:Also, if $f$ is a polynomial, $\LM(f)$ denotes its leading monomial for that order. If $I$  is an ideal, then $\LM(I)$ denotes the ideal generated by the leading monomials of all polynomials in $I$.
1:\begin{defn}
2:  Let $(f_1,\ldots, f_m)$ be 
3:  polynomials in $R$. The \emph{boolean Macaulay
4:  matrix} in degree~$d$ (denoted by $\mathsf{Macaulay}(d)$) is the matrix
5:  whose rows contain the coefficients of the polynomials $\{\varphi(t
6:  f_j)\}$ where $1\leq j\leq m$, $t$ is a squarefree monomial, and $\deg(t f_j)=d$. The columns
7: correspond to the
8:  squarefree monomials in $R$ of degree at most $d$ and are ordered in descending order with respect to the grevlex ordering. The element in the row corresponding to $\varphi(t f_j)$ and the
9:  column corresponding to the monomial $m$ is the coefficient of $m$
10:  in the polynomial $\varphi(t f_j)$.
11:\end{defn}
13:Note that the boolean Macaulay matrix can be obtained as a submatrix
14:of the classical Macaulay matrix of the system $\langle
15:f_1,\ldots,f_m, x_1^2-x_1,\ldots, x_n^2-x_n\rangle$ after Gaussian reduction by
16:the rows corresponding to the polynomials $(x_1^2-x_1,\ldots,
17:x_n^2-x_n)$.
19:\begin{lem}\label{lem:inconsistent}
20:  Let $\mathsf M$ be the $r_{\mathsf{Mac}}\times c_{\mathsf{Mac}}$ boolean Macaulay matrix of the system $(f_1,\ldots,
21:  f_m)$ in degree $d$. 
22:  Let $\mathbf r$
23:  denote the $1\times c_{\mathsf{Mac}}$ vector 
24:$\mathbf r=(0,\dots,0,1)$.
25:  If the linear system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$ has a solution,
26:  then the system $f_1=\dots = f_m=0$ has no solution in $\mathbb{F}_2^n$.
27:\end{lem}
29:\begin{proof}
30:If the system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$ has a solution, then there exists a linear combination of the rows of the Macaulay matrix which yields the constant polynomial $1$. Therefore, $1\in\langle f_1,\ldots, f_m,x_1^2-x_1,\ldots, x_n^2-x_n\rangle$.
31:\end{proof}
32:\subsection{Witness degree}
33:We consider an indicator of the 
34:complexity of affine polynomial systems: the \emph{witness degree}. It
35:has the property that a Gr\"obner basis of the ideal generated by the
36:polynomials can be obtained by performing linear algebra on the
37:Macaulay matrix in this degree. In particular, if the system has no
38:solution, then the witness degree is closely related to the classical
39:\emph{effective
40:  Nullstellensatz} (see e.g., \cite{Jelonek2005}). 
45:\begin{defn}\label{defn:dlin}
46:Let $\mathbf F=(f_1,\ldots, f_m, x_1^2-x_1,\ldots, x_n^2-x_n)$ be a sequence of polynomials and $I=\langle \mathbf F\rangle$ the ideal it generates.
47:Denote by $I_{\leq d}$ and by $J_{\leq d}$ the $\mathbb{F}_2$-vector spaces defined by
48:$$\begin{array}{rccl}
49:I_{\leq d}&=&\{p \mid& p\in I, \deg(p)\leq d\},\\
50:J_{\leq d}&=&\{p \mid& \exists h_1,\ldots, h_{m+n}, \forall i\in \{1,\ldots, m+n\}, \deg(h_i)\leq d-2,\\
51:&&&p=\sum_{i=1}^m h_i f_i + \sum_{j=1}^n h_{m+j} (x_j^2-x_j)\}.\end{array}$$
53:\noindent We call \emph{witness degree} $(\dwit)$ of $\mathbf F$ the smallest integer $d_0$ such that $I_{\leq d_0}=J_{\leq d_0}$ and $\langle\{\LM(f) \mid f\in I_{\leq d_0}\}\rangle=\LM(I)$.
54:\end{defn}
56:Consider a row echelon form of the boolean Macaulay matrix in degree $d$ of
57:the system $(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ of polynomials. Then the first nonzero element of each row corresponds to a leading monomial of an element of~$I$, belonging to~$\LM(I)$. For large enough $d$,
58:Dickson's lemma \cite[\S2.4, Thm. 5]{CoxLitShe97} implies that the collection of those monomials up to degree~$d$ generates~$\LM(I)$ and thus the polynomials corresponding to those rows together with
59:$\{x_1^2-x_1,\ldots, x_n^2-x_n\}$ form a Gr\"obner basis of~$I$
60:with
61:respect to the grevlex ordering. Another interpretation of the \emph{witness degree} is that it is precisely the smallest such $d$.
1:\begin{algorithm}
2:\caption{BooleanSolve}
3:\label{algo:booleansolve}
4:\begin{algorithmic}[1]
5:\Require $m, n, k\in\mathbb N$ such that $m\geq n>k$ and $f_1,\ldots,f_m$ quadratic polynomials in $\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n]$.
6:\Ensure The set of boolean solutions of the system $f_1=\dots=f_m=0$.
7:\State $S:=\emptyset$. 
8:\State ${d_{0}}:=$ index of the first nonpositive coefficient in the series expansion at~0 of the rational function $\frac{(1+t)^{n-k}}{(1-t)(1+t^2)^{m}}$.
9:\ForAll{$(a_{n-k+1},\ldots, a_n)\in\mathbb F_2^{k}$}
10:\For{$i$ from $1$ to $m$}
11:\State $\tilde{f}_i(x_1,\ldots,x_{n-k}):=f_i(x_1,\ldots, x_{n-k},a_{n-k+1},\ldots, a_n)\in \mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_{n-k}]$.
12:\EndFor 
13:\State $\mathsf{M}:=$ boolean Macaulay matrix of $(\tilde{f}_1,\ldots,\tilde{f}_m)$ in degree ${d_{0}}$.
14:\If{the system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$ is inconsistent}\Comment{$\mathbf r$ as defined in Lemma~\ref{lem:inconsistent}}
15:\State $T:=$ solutions of the system ($\tilde{f}_1=\cdots=\tilde{f}_m=0$) (exhaustive search).
16:\ForAll{$(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k})\in T$}
17:\State $S:=S\cup \{(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k},a_{n-k+1},\ldots, a_n)\}$.
18:\EndFor
19:\EndIf
20:\EndFor
21:\State \Return $S$.
22:\end{algorithmic}
23:\end{algorithm}
25:Our algorithm is given in Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolve}.
26:The general principle is to perform an exhaustive search in two steps,
27:using a test of consistency of the Macaulay matrix to prune most of the
28:branches of the second step of the search.
31:When the system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$ is
32:consistent, the corresponding branch of the searching tree is not explored.
33:In that case, by Lemma~\ref{lem:inconsistent}, any solution of the linear system $\mathbf u\cdot \mathsf
34:M=\mathbf r$ can be used as a certificate that there exists no solution of the
35:polynomial system $f_1=\dots=f_m=0$ in this branch.
38:\begin{prop}\label{algo_correct}Algorithm~{\sf{BooleanSolve}} is correct and solves the Boolean MQ\ problem.
39:\end{prop}
40:\begin{proof}
41:By Lemma~\ref{lem:inconsistent}, if the test in line~8 finds the linear system to be consistent, then there can be no solution with the given values of~$(a_{n-k+1},\dots,a_n)$. Otherwise, the exhaustive search proceeds and cannot miss a solution. It is important to note that the choice of the actual value~${d}_0$ does not have any impact on the correctness of the algorithm.
42:\end{proof}
44:Algorithm~{\sf{BooleanSolve}} is easily be adapted to solve the Boolean MQ\  SAT problem by replacing
45:lines 9-12 of the previous algorithm by:
46:\begin{algorithm}[H]
47:\begin{algorithmic}[1]
48:\setcounter{ALG@line}{8}
49:\State $T:=$ at least one solution of the system ($\tilde{f}_1=\cdots=\tilde{f}_m=0$) (modified exhaustive search).
50:\If{\(T\neq\emptyset\)} 
52:\State  \Return \(\{(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k},a_{n-k+1},\ldots, a_n)\mid
53:(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k})\in T
54:\}\)
55:\EndIf
56:\end{algorithmic}
57:\end{algorithm}
58: 
1:\label{sec:wiedemann}
2:The choice of the algorithm to test whether the sparse linear system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$
3:is consistent or not is crucial for the efficiency of Algorithm {\sf BooleanSolve}. 
4:A simple deterministic algorithm consists in computing a row echelon form of the matrix: the linear system is consistent if and only if the last nonzero row of the row echelon form is equal to the vector $\mathbf r$.
5:We show in Section~\ref{sec:compl} that this is sufficient to pass below the $2^n$ complexity barrier. 
6:We recall for future use the complexity of this method.
7:\begin{prop}[\cite{Storjohann2000}, Proposition 2.11]\label{prop:row_ech} The row echelon form of an $N\times M$ matrix over a field~$k$ can be computed in~$O(NMr^{\theta-2})$ arithmetic operations in~$k$, where~$r$ is the rank of the matrix and $\theta\le3$ is such that any two~$n\times n$ matrices over~$k$ can be multiplied in~$O(n^\theta)$ arithmetic operations in~$k$.
8:\end{prop}
9:Here, $\theta=3$ is the cost of classical matrix multiplication and in this case a simple Gaussian reduction to row echelon form is sufficient. The best known value for $\theta$ has been~2.376 for a long time, by a result of~\cite{CoWi90}. Recent improvements of that method by~\cite{Stothers10,VassilevskaWilliams11} have decreased it to~2.3727, but this does not have a significant impact on our analysis.
11:This result does not exploit the sparsity of Macaulay matrices. We do not know of an efficient deterministic algorithm for row reduction that exploits this sparsity. 
12:Instead, we use an efficient Las Vegas variant of Wiedemann's algorithm due to \cite{GieLobSau98}, whose 
13:specification is summarized in Algorithm~{\sf TestConsistency}. In this algorithm, the matrix~$A$ is given by two black boxes performing the operations~$x\mapsto Ax$ and $u\mapsto A^tu$. The complexity is expressed in terms of the number of evaluations of these black boxes, which in our context will each have a cost bounded by the number of nonzero coefficients of Macaulay matrices. The algorithm is presented in \citep{GieLobSau98} for matrices with entries in an arbitrary field. We specialize it here in the case where the field is $\mathbb{F}_2$. The key ideas are a preconditioning of the matrix by multiplying it by random Toeplitz matrices and working in a suitable field extension to get access to sufficiently many points for picking random elements.
16:\begin{algorithm}
17:\caption{TestConsistency \citep{GieLobSau98}}
18:\label{algo:testinconsistency}
19:\label{alg:sparsesolver}
20:\begin{algorithmic}[1]
21:\Require\begin{itemize}
22:\item A black box for $\mathbf x\mapsto \mathsf A\cdot \mathbf x$, where $\mathsf A\in \mathbb K^{N\times N}$.
23:\item A black box for $\mathbf u\mapsto \mathsf A^t\cdot\mathbf u$.
24:\item $\mathbf b\in \mathbb K^{N\times 1}$.
25:\end{itemize}
26:\Ensure \begin{itemize}
27:\item (``consistent'',$\mathbf x$) with $\mathsf A\cdot \mathbf x=\mathbf b$ if the system has a solution
28:\item (``inconsistent'',$\mathbf u$) if the system does not have a solution, with $\mathbf u^t\cdot\mathsf A=0$ and $\mathbf u^t\cdot\mathbf b\neq 0$, certifying the inconsistency.
29:\end{itemize}
30:\end{algorithmic}
31:\end{algorithm}
33:\begin{prop}[\cite{GieLobSau98}]\label{thm:wiedemann}
34:Algorithm \ref{alg:sparsesolver} 
35:determines the consistency of an $N\times N$ matrix with expected
36:complexity $O(N \log N )$ evaluations of the black boxes and 
37:$O(N^2\log^2N\log\log N)$ additional operations in
38:$\mathbb{F}_2$. 
39:\end{prop}
41:Macaulay matrices are rectangular. We therefore first make them square by padding with zeroes. The complexity estimate is then used with~$N$ the maximum of the row and column dimensions of the matrices.

