4:\centerline{\includegraphics[width=.7\textwidth]{fig_proba4bis}}
5:\caption{Proportion of specialized quadratic systems for which the
6:  linear system (line 9 of Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolve}) is
7:  consistent. Parameters: $k=\left\lceil 1/2+n-\frac{\sqrt{-7+8
8:        n}}{2}\right\rceil$. In red, $\delta=0$ (corresponding to
9:  Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolve}); in green, $\delta=1$ (see
10:  Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolvev} of Section \ref{sec:BoolDelta}).
11:\label{fig:proba}}
12:\end{minipage}
13:\end{figure}
16:We showed in Section \ref{sec:numBeta} that when the first nonpositive
17:coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{n-k,n}$ is close to zero, then the linear
18:filtering may not be as efficient as expected (for instance in the
19:case $\gamma=.55$, $n=23$ in Figure \ref{fig:strongsemireg}). Another
20:case is shown in Figure \ref{fig:proba}. The curve $\delta=0$ shows
21:the behavior of Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolve} on random square
22:systems ($m=n$) where $k$ is chosen as small as possible such that the
23:witness degree is $\dwit=2$: this is obtained by choosing
24:$k=\left\lceil 1/2+n-\frac{\sqrt{-7+8 n}}{2}\right\rceil$ (that is
25:$d_0=2$).
28:First, we observe that specializing a uniformly distributed random quadratic polynomial
29:$P\in\mathbb F_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n]$ at a uniformly distributed random point in $\mathbb
30:F_2^k$ yields a random polynomial that is also uniformly distributed 
31:in $\mathbb F_2[x_1,\ldots, x_{n-k}]$. We assume here that $P$ is reduced modulo the field equations $\langle
32:x_1^2-x_1,\ldots, x_n^2-x_n\rangle$. Let us assume first that
33:$k=1$. Then $P$ can be rewritten as
34:$$P(x_1,\ldots,x_n)=x_n P_1(x_1,\ldots, x_{n-1})+ P_2(x_1,\ldots, x_{n-1}),$$
35:where $P_1$ (resp. $P_2$) is a random polynomial following a uniform
36:distribution on the set of reduced boolean polynomials of degree $1$
37:(resp. of degree $2$) in $\mathbb F_2[x_1,\ldots, x_{n-1}]$.
38:Therefore, if $a\in \mathbb F_2$ is a random variable, $P(x_1,\ldots,
39:x_{n-1},a)\in \mathbb F_2[x_1,\ldots, x_{n-1}]$ is either $P_1$ or
40:$P_1+P_2$ and thus follows a uniform distribution on the set of
41:reduced quadratic boolean polynomials. The extension to arbitrary~$k<n$ follows by induction.
43:Consequently, in the special case $d_0=2$ of Figure \ref{fig:proba}
44:the boolean Macaulay matrix of a specialized system will be uniformly
45:distributed among the boolean matrices with the same dimensions. Also,
46:due to the choice of $k$, it will be roughly square. However, in
47:$\mathbb F_2$, the probability that a random square matrix has full
48:rank is not close to $1$. An estimate of this probability can be
49:obtained as follows.
51:The probability that a
52:random $p\times q$ boolean matrix has rank $r$ is (see \cite{FisherAlexander1966,Stitzinger87})
53:$$P(p,q,r)=2^{-p q}\displaystyle\frac{\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(2^p-2^j)\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(2^q-2^j)}{\prod_{j=0}^{r-1}(2^r-2^j)}.$$
55:Therefore, given a nonzero vector $\mathbf v\in \mathbb F_2^p$ and a random boolean $p\times q$ matrix $\mathsf M$, the probability that the linear system $\mathbf u\cdot \mathsf M = \mathbf v$ is consistent is
56:$$Q(p,q)=\sum_{i=1}^{p} P(p,q,i)\left(\frac{2^{i}-1}{2^q-1}\right).$$
58:Direct numerical computations show that for square matrices,
59:$Q(p,p)\approx 0.61$ as soon as $p\geq 4$. This probability
60:corresponds to the valleys of the curve $\delta=0$ in Figure~\ref{fig:proba}.  Also,
61:it can be noticed that $Q(p,q)$ grows quickly with $p-q$. For
62:instance, $Q(p+6,p)\approx 0.99$ when $p\geq 1$.
63: 
64:Consequently, it is interesting to specialize more variables than $k$ in
65:some cases (especially when the first nonpositive coefficient of ${(1+t)^{n-k}}/({(1-t)(1+t^2)^{m}})$ has small
66:absolute value): doing so increases the difference between the
67:dimensions of the Macaulay matrices. This does not change the
68:correctness of the algorithm (nor its asymptotic complexity), but
69:increases the effectiveness of the filtering performed by linear
70:algebra.
72:\subsection{Improving the quality of the filtering for small values
73:of \(n\)}
74:\label{sec:BoolDelta}
75:In this section, we propose an extension of Algorithm
76:\textsf{BooleanSolve} which takes an extra argument $\delta$, in order
77:to avoid the behavior of the algorithm shown in Section~\ref{sec:proba}. The main idea is to specialize $k+\delta$ variables,
78:but to take only $k$ into account for the computation of
79:$d_0$. Consequently, the difference between the number of columns and
80:the rank of the Macaulay matrix is not too small, and hence the linear
81:filtering performs better.
82:The resulting algorithm is given in Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolvev}.
84:\begin{algorithm}
85:\caption{improved BooleanSolve.}
86:\label{algo:booleansolvev}
87:\begin{algorithmic}[1]
88:\Require $m, n, k, \delta\in\mathbb N$ such that $k+\delta<n\leq m$ and $f_1,\ldots,f_m$ quadratic polynomials in $\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\ldots, x_n]$.
89:\Ensure The set of boolean solutions of the system $f_1=\dots=f_m=0$.
90:\State $S:=\emptyset$. 
91:\State ${d_{0}}:=$ index of the first nonpositive coefficient in the series expansion of the rational function $\frac{(1+t)^{n-k}}{(1-t)(1+t^2)^{m}}$.
92:\ForAll{$(a_{n-k-\delta+1},\ldots, a_n)\in\mathbb F_2^{k+\delta}$}
93:\For{$i$ from $1$ to $m$}
94:\State $\tilde{f}_i(x_1,\ldots,x_{n-k-\delta}):=f_i(x_1,\ldots, x_{n-k-\delta},a_{n-k-\delta+1},\ldots, a_n)$.
95:\EndFor 
96:\State $\mathsf{M}:=$ boolean Macaulay matrix of $(\tilde{f}_1,\ldots,\tilde{f}_m)$ in degree ${d_{0}}$.
97:\If{the system $\mathbf u\cdot\mathsf M=\mathbf r$ is inconsistent}\Comment{$\mathbf r$ as defined in Lemma~\ref{lem:inconsistent}}
98:\State $T:=$ solutions of the system ($\tilde{f}_1=\cdots=\tilde{f}_m=0$) (exhaustive search).
99:\ForAll{$(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k-\delta})\in T$}
100:\State $S:=S\cup \{(t_1,\ldots, t_{n-k-\delta},a_{n-k-\delta+1},\ldots, a_n)\}$.
101:\EndFor
102:\EndIf
103:\EndFor
104:\State \Return $S$.
105:\end{algorithmic}
106:\end{algorithm}
109:In Figure \ref{fig:proba},
110: we show the role of the parameter $\delta$
111:when $k$ is chosen minimal such that $d_0=2$: adding redundancy by
112:choosing a nonzero $\delta$ can greatly improve the quality of the
113:filtering (in practice, choosing $\delta=1$ is sufficient). 
116:\begin{figure}[H]
117:\centerline{
118:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg045Delta1}}
119:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg073Delta1}}}
120:\caption{Quality of the filtering with $\delta=1$.\label{fig:deltasup0}}
121:\end{figure}
123:Figure \ref{fig:deltasup0} shows further experimental evidence that adding redundancy by choosing $\delta=1$ permits to avoid problems occurring when the first nonpositive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{n-k,m}$ is close to zero. For instance, the peak at $\gamma=.55$, $n=23$ that appeared in Figure \ref{fig:strongsemireg} disappears when $\delta=1$. 
1:In some cases, when the boolean system is \emph{not random}, the
2:choice of $d_0$ proposed in Algorithm \textsf{BooleanSolve} may be
3:too large. This happens for instance for systems that have inner
4:structure, which has an impact on the algebraic structure of the ideal
5:generated by the polynomials. Examples of such structure can be found
6:in Cryptology, for instance with boolean systems coming for the HFE
7:cryptosystem \citep{Pat96}, as shown in~\citep{FauJou03}.
9:For these systems, the choice of $d_0$ as the index of the first
10:non-positive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{n,m}$ would be very
11:pessimistic, since the Macaulay matrices in degree $d_0$ would be
12:larger than necessary. However, if estimates of the witness degree are
13:known (this is the case for HFE), then $d_0$ can be chosen accordingly
14:as a parameter of the Algorithm \textsf{BooleanSolve}.
1:\subsection{Application in Cryptology}
3:Careful implementation of the algorithm will be necessary to
4:estimate accurately the efficiency of the {\sf BooleanSolve} algorithm.
5:For instance a crucial operation is the Wiedemann (or block Wiedemann)
6:algorithm; in practice, it is probably useless to work in a field
7:extension \(\mathbb{F}_{2^{k}}\) as requested by 
8:Proposition~\ref{thm:wiedemann}.
9:Working directly over \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\) and packing several
10:elements (bits) into words may have a dramatic effect on the
11:constant hidden in Theorem~\ref{thm:globalcompl}. In the following
12:we estimate the impact of the new algorithm from the point of
13:view of a user in Cryptology. In other words, if the security
14:of a cryptosystem relies on the hardness of solving a polynomial
15:system, by how much must the parameters be increased to keep the
16:same level of security?
18:The stream cipher QUAD~\citep{QUADEuro,QUADJSC} enjoys a provable security argument to support  its conjectured strength.  It relies on the iteration of a set of overdetermined multivariate quadratic polynomials over  \(\mathbb{F}_{2}\)
19:so that the security of the keystream generation is related, in the concrete security model, to the difficulty  of solving the Boolean MQ SAT\ problem. A theoretical bound is used in~\citep{QUADJSC}
20:to obtain secure parameters for a given security
21:bound \(T\) and a given maximal length $L$ of the keystream sequence that can be generated with a pair (key, IV): for instance (see~\cite{QUADJSC}
22:p. 1711), for \(T=2^{80}, L=2^{40}, k=2\) and an advantage of more than \(\varepsilon=1/100\), the bound gives
23:\(n\geq 331\). We report in the following table various values
24:of \(n\) depending on \(L\), \(T\) and \(\varepsilon\):
26:    \begin{center}
27:    \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}\hline
28:T & L & \(\varepsilon\) & n \\\hline
29:$2^{80}$ &   $2^{40}$ &   1/100    &       331\\\hline
30:$2^{80}$ &   $2^{22}$ &   1/100    &       253\\\hline
31:$2^{160}$ &  $2^{80}$ &   1/100    &       613\\\hline
32:$2^{160}$ &  $2^{40}$ &   1/100    &       445\\\hline
33:$2^{160}$ &  $2^{40}$ &   1/1000   &       448\\\hline
34:$2^{160}$ &  $2^{40}$ &   1/10000  &       467\\\hline
35:$2^{256}$ &  $2^{40}$ &   1/100    &       584\\\hline
36:$2^{256}$ &  $2^{80}$ &   1/100    &       758\\\hline
37:\end{tabular}\\
38:\vspace*{2mm}
39:Security parameters for the stream cipher QUAD~\citep{QUADJSC}
40:\end{center}
42:Now, the question is to achieve a security bound for \(T=2^{256}\); what are the minimal values
43:of \(m\) and \(n\) ensuring that solving the Boolean MQ SAT
44:requires at least \(T\) bit-operations? Using the complexity
45:analysis of the BooleanSolve algorithm we can derive useful
46: lower bounds for \(n\) when \(m=n\) or \(m=2\,n\)
47:(\(m=2\,n\) corresponds to the recommended parameters for QUAD). In
48:the following table we report the corresponding values: 
49: \begin{center}
50: \begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\hline
51:Security Bound \(T\) & \rule{0cm}{5mm}$2^{128}$ & $2^{256}$ & $2^{512}$ & $2^{1024}$ \\\hline
52:Minimal value of \(n\) when \(m=n\)& \(128\) & \(270\) & \(576\) & \text{1202} \\\hline
53:Minimal value of \(n\) when \(m=2\,n\)& \(145\) & \(335\) & \(738\) & 1580 \\\hline
54:\end{tabular}
55:\end{center}
57:Comparing with exhaustive search we can see from this table that:
58:\begin{itemize}
59:\item our algorithm does not improve upon exhaustive search when \(n\)
60:  is small (for instance when \(m=n\) and \(T=2^{128}\) that are the
61:  recommended parameters);
62:\item by contrast, our algorithm can take advantage of the
63:  overdeterminedness of the algebraic systems: this explains why the
64:  values we recommend are larger than expected when \(n\) is large
65:  and/or \(m/n>1\).
66:\end{itemize}

