1:We now estimate the complexity of Algorithm~{\sf BooleanSolve} by
2:going through its steps and making all necessary hypotheses
3:explicit. We consider the case when the number of variables $n$ and
4:the number of polynomials $m$ are related by~$m\sim\alpha n$ for some
5:$\alpha\ge1$ and $n$ is large. Also we assume that the ratio $k/n$ is
6:controlled by a parameter $\gamma\in [0,1]$, i.e., $k=
7:(1-\gamma)n$.
9:The first step (lines 4 to 6 in the algorithm) is to evaluate the polynomials~$\tilde{f}_i$ from the polynomials~$f_i$. With no arithmetic operations, the polynomials~$f_i$ can first be written as polynomials in~$(x_1,\dots,x_{n-k})$ with coefficients that are polynomials of degree at most~2 in~$x_{n-k+1},\dots,x_n$ and at most~1 in each variable. Each such coefficient has at most $1+k+\binom{k}{2}$ monomials, each of which can be evaluated with at most one arithmetic operation. 
10:The total number of these polynomial coefficients is at most~$m(1+n-k+\binom{n-k}{2})$. Thus the total cost of all the evaluations of the coefficients of the polynomials~$\tilde{f}_i$ is at most~$O(n^52^{(1-\gamma) n})$. This turns out to be asymptotically negligible compared to the next steps.
12:The next stage (line~8) of our algorithm consists in performing tests of inconsistency of the Macaulay matrices.
13:\begin{prop}\label{prop:complFirstStep}For any $\epsilon>0$, $\alpha\ge 1$ and
14:  sufficiently large $m=\lceil\alpha n\rceil$, the complexity of all tests of
15:  consistency of Macaulay matrices in Algorithm
16:  \textsf{BooleanSolve} with parameters~$(m,n,k)$ is
17:        \begin{itemize}
18:                \item $O(2^{(1-\gamma+\theta F_{\alpha}(\gamma)+\epsilon)n})$ in the deterministic variant; 
19:                \item of expectation $O(2^{(1-\gamma+2 F_{\alpha}(\gamma)+\epsilon)n})$ in the probabilistic variant,
20:        \end{itemize}
21:where $\gamma=1-k/n$, $F_{\alpha}(\gamma)=-\gamma\log_2(D^D(1-D)^{1-D})$ with $D=M(\alpha/\gamma)$, the function $M$ as in Proposition~\ref{prop:dregh} and $\theta$ the complexity of linear algebra as in Proposition~\ref{prop:row_ech}.
22:\end{prop}
23:A notable feature of this result is that in terms of complexity, the probabilistic variant of our algorithm behaves as the deterministic one where the linear algebra would be performed in quadratic complexity (i.e., with $\theta=2$).
24:\begin{proof}
25:We first estimate the size of the Macaulay matrices. 
26:  By Proposition~\ref{prop:dregh}, the index~$d_0$, which
27:  is $1+\deg(\mathsf{HS}_{n-k,m})$ behaves
28:  asymptotically like $\gamma Dn$. The function $M(x)$ is decreasing
29:  for $x\ge 1$, so that $D\le M(1)<1/2$. Thus, $d_0<\gamma n/2$ for
30:  $n$ sufficiently large and Proposition~\ref{prop:sizemac} applies
31:  with~$d=d_0$, $m=\lceil\alpha n\rceil$ equations and $n-k=\gamma n$
32:  variables. For~$n$ sufficiently large, the bound
33:  for~$r_{\mathsf{Mac}}$ is larger than that
34:  for~$c_{\mathsf{Mac}}$, since 
35:  the quotient of these two bounds is
36:  ${m}/{(\frac {\gamma n} {d_0} - 1)^2}$, which grows linearly with~$n$.
38:Next, we turn to the tests of inconsistency. The previous bounds and Proposition~\ref{prop:row_ech} imply that
39:  the number of operations required for the computation of the row
40:  echelon form is
41:  $O(n\binom{\gamma n}{d_0}^{\theta})$. Similarly, by Proposition
42:  \ref{thm:wiedemann}, the complexity of checking the consistency of
43:  each matrix by the probabilistic method is
44: $O(r_{\mathsf{Mac}}\log(r_{\mathsf{Mac}})s_{\mathsf{Mac}})=O(n^4\binom{\gamma n}{d_0}^2\log{\binom{\gamma n}{d_0}})$
45:  and that bound dominates the cost of the additional operations
46:  in~$\mathbb{F}_2$. Now, Stirling's formula implies that for
47:  any~$0<b<a$, $\log\binom{an}{bn}\sim
48:  n\log(a^a/(b^b(a-b)^{a-b}))$. Setting~$a=\gamma$ and $b=\gamma D$
49:  gives the result, the extra factor being due to the exhaustive
50:  search that performs this consistency check $2^{(1-\gamma)n}$ times.
51:\end{proof}
52:In the cases where the linear system $\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathsf{M}=\mathbf{r}$ is found inconsistent, then the polynomial system itself may be consistent and the algorithm proceeds with an exhaustive search (line~9) in a system with $\gamma n$ unknowns.
53:Each such search has cost $O(2^{(\gamma+\epsilon)n})$. As long as the number of these searches does not exceed $O(2^{(1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma))n})$, the overall complexity of the algorithm is bounded by the complexity given in Proposition~\ref{prop:complFirstStep}. There can be two causes for the inconsistency of the linear system that triggers such a search: the existence of an actual solution with~$x_{n}=a_{n},\dots,x_{n-k+1}=a_{n-k+1}$; a witness degree of the specialized system larger than ${d}_0$ (e.g., if the homogenized specialized system is not boolean semi-regular). 
54:We now define a class of systems where this does not happen too much.
56:\begin{defn} \label{defn:strongsemireg}Let $S=(f_1,\ldots,f_m)$ be quadratic polynomials in $\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\dots, x_n]$, $0\le k=(1-\gamma)n<n$, $\alpha= m/n$ and $d_0=1+\deg(\mathsf{HS}_{n-k,m})$. The system~$S$ is called $\gamma$-strong semi-regular if both the set of its solutions in~${\mathbb{F}_2^n}$ and the set              
57:\begin{multline*}
58:\bigl\{(a_{n-k+1},\dots, a_n)\in \mathbb F_2^k\mid \\ \dwit(f_1(x_1,\dots,x_{n-k},a_{n-k+1},\dots,a_n),\dots,f_m(x_1,\dots,x_{n-k},a_{n-k+1},\dots,a_n))> d_0\bigr\}
59:\end{multline*}
60:have cardinality at most ${2^{(1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma))n}}$, with $F_{\alpha}$ as in Proposition~\ref{prop:complFirstStep}.
61:\end{defn}
62:Note that since $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)$ is a decreasing function of~$\gamma$, a $\gamma$-strong semi-regular system is also $\gamma'$-strong semi-regular for any $\gamma'<\gamma$. 
64:The first condition for a system to be $\gamma$-strong semi-regular concerns its number of solutions. For boolean systems drawn uniformly at random, it is known that the probability that the number of boolean solutions is~$s$ decreases more than exponentially with~$s$ \citep{FusBac07}, so that the first condition is fulfilled with large probability. 
65:The second condition is related to the proportion of boolean semi-regular systems. We discuss this condition in the next section and show that it is also of large probability experimentally. Under this assumption of $\gamma$-strong semi-regularity, we now state the complexity of the algorithm obtained by optimizing the choice of the number~$k$ of variables that are specialized.
67:We first discuss large values of~$\gamma$. The function $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)$ is decreasing with~$\alpha$
68:and negative when $\gamma=1$. Thus, the first condition implies that a $1$-strong semi-regular system has no solution. By continuity, this behavior persists for $\gamma$ close to~1 and actually holds for~$\gamma\in(0.824,1)$. It also persists for smaller values of $\gamma$ and larger $\alpha$.
70:\begin{cor}\label{cor:inconsistent}With the same notations as in Prop.~\ref{prop:complFirstStep}, when a system is $\gamma$-strong semi-regular with $\alpha$ and $\gamma$ such that $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)<0$, then it is inconsistent and detected by Algorithm {\sf BooleanSolve} with parameters~$(m,n,0)$ in $O(2^{(\theta F_{\alpha}(1)+\epsilon)n})$ operations.
71:\end{cor}
72:\begin{figure}
73:  \centering
74:\input{graphTheta/exponent.tex}
75:  \caption{Exponent of the complexity for inconsistent systems in terms of the ratio $\alpha$ (see Thm. \ref{thm:globalcompl} and Cor.~\ref{cor:inconsistent})}
76:  \label{fig:exponent}
77:\end{figure}
78:\noindent The value of the exponent
79:$\theta F_\alpha(1)$ in terms of $\alpha$ is plotted in
80:Figure~\ref{fig:exponent} (it corresponds to the right part of the plots, i.e. $\alpha>1.82$ for $\theta=2$, $\alpha>2.48$ for $\theta=2.376$, $\alpha>3.64$ for $\theta=3$).
81:\begin{proof}The hypothesis implies that the system has no solution and that its witness degree is bounded by~$d_0$, so that its absence of solution is detected by the linear algebra step in degree~$d_0$. In that case, no exhaustive search is needed. 
82:\end{proof}
83: 
84:For smaller values of~$\gamma$, the algorithm requires exhaustive searches. The optimal choice of~$k$ is obtained by an optimization on the complexity estimate. This leads to the following complexity estimates. In the next section, we argue that the required strong semi-regularities are very likely in practice, so that the only choice left to the user is that of the linear algebra routine.
86:\begin{thm}\label{thm:globalcompl}
87:Let $S=(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ be a system of quadratic polynomials in $\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\dots, x_n]$, with $m=\lceil \alpha n\rceil$ and $\alpha\ge1$. 
88:Then Algorithm~\textsf{BooleanSolve} finds all its roots in $\mathbb{F}_2^n$ with a number of arithmetic operations in~$\mathbb{F}_2$ that is
89:\begin{itemize}
90:        \item $O(2^{(1-0.112\alpha) n})$ if $S$ is $(.27\alpha)$-strong semi-regular using Gaussian elimination for the linear algebra step;
91:        \item $O(2^{(1-0.159\alpha) n})$ if $S$ is $(.40\alpha)$-strong semi-regular using computation of the row echelon form with Coppersmith-Winograd multiplication;
92:        \item of expectation $O(2^{(1-0.208\alpha) n})$ if $S$ is $(.55\alpha)$-strong semi-regular using the probabilistic Algorithm~\ref{algo:testinconsistency}.
93:\end{itemize}
94:In all cases, the value of~$k$ passed to the algorithm is $\lceil n(1-\gamma)\rceil$ with $\gamma$ corresponding to the strong semi-regularity.
95:\end{thm}
96:\begin{proof}The correctness of the algorithm has already been proved in Proposition~\ref{algo_correct}. Only the complexity remains to be proved.
98:  By definition of strong semi-regularity, the number of exhaustive
99:  searches that need be performed in line~9 of the Algorithm
100:  is~$O(2^{(1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma))n})$, each of them
101:  using~$O(2^{(\gamma+\epsilon)n})$ arithmetic operations for
102:  any~$\epsilon>0$. It follows that the overall cost of these
103:  exhaustive searches
104:  is~$O(2^{(1-\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)+\epsilon)n})$; it is
105:  bounded by the cost of the tests of inconsistency. We now choose
106:  $\gamma$ in such a way as to minimize this cost, in terms of
107:  $\alpha$. Direct computations lead to the following numerical
108:  results, that conclude the proof.
109:\end{proof}
111:\begin{lem} With the same notation as in Proposition~\ref{prop:complFirstStep}, the function $1-\gamma+\theta F_{\alpha}(\gamma)$ is bounded by
112:        \begin{itemize}
113:        \item $1 - 0.112\alpha $ when $\theta=3$ and $\gamma=0.27\alpha$;
114:        \item $1 - 0.159\alpha$ when $\theta=2.376$ and $\gamma=0.40\alpha$;
115:                \item $1 - 0.208\alpha$ when $\theta=2$ and $\gamma=0.55\alpha$.
116:        \end{itemize}
117:\end{lem}
118:\begin{proof}
119:The function $1-\gamma + \theta F_{\alpha}(\gamma)$ has two parameters but its extrema can be found by reducing it to a one parameter function. Indeed, this function is maximal for $\alpha\ge1$ and $\gamma\in[0,1]$ when $(-\gamma+\theta F_\alpha(\gamma))/\alpha$ is. Setting $\lambda=\gamma/\alpha$, this is exactly $-\lambda+\theta F_1(\lambda)$, with $\lambda\in[0,1/\alpha]$.
120:Direct computations lead to the optimal $\lambda$'s:
121:$\lambda=\min(1/\alpha,0.27)$ when $\theta=3$, $\lambda=\min(1/\alpha,0.40)$ when $\theta=2.376$, $\lambda=\min(1/\alpha,0.55)$ when $\theta=2$.
122:\end{proof}
1:\section{Numerical Experiments on Random Systems}
2:\label{sec:expmodel}
4:\textbf{Probabilistic model.} In this section, we study experimentally the behavior of Algorithm
5:\textsf{BooleanSolve} of random quadratic systems where each
6:coefficient is 0 or 1 with probability 1/2. These random boolean quadratic
7:systems appear naturally in Cryptology since the security of several
8:recent cryptosystems relies directly on the difficulty of solving such
9:systems (see e.g., \cite{QUADEuro,QUADJSC}).
11:\subsection{$\gamma$-strong semi-regularity}
12:\label{sec:numBeta}
15:The goal of this section is to give experimental evidence that the
16:assumption of $\gamma$-strong semi-regularity is not a strong condition
17:for random boolean systems. This is related to the notoriously difficult
18:conjecture by \cite{Fro85}, which states that in characteristic $0$,
19:almost all systems are semi-regular (with the meaning of
20:semi-regularity given in \citep{BarFauSal05}), see also \citep{Moreno-Socias2003}.
22:Consequently, we propose the following conjecture, which can be seen
23:as a variant of Fr\"oberg's conjecture for boolean systems:
25:\begin{conj}\label{conj:propbeta}For any~$\alpha\ge 1$ and $\gamma<1$ such that
26:  $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)>0$, the proportion of $\gamma$-strong
27:  semi-regular systems of $\lceil\alpha n\rceil$ quadratic
28:  polynomials in~$\mathbb{F}_2[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ tends to~1
29:  when~$n\rightarrow\infty$.
30:\end{conj}
32:The rest of this section is devoted to providing experiments supporting this conjecture.
34:\begin{figure}
35:\centerline{
36:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg010.pdf}}
37:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg019.pdf}}}
38:\centerline{
39:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg045.pdf}}
40:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg073.pdf}}}
41:\centerline{
42:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{figStrongReg090.pdf}}}
43:\caption{Relation between the quality of the filtering, the value of
44:  the first nonpositive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma
45:    n\rfloor,n}$, and $\gamma$-strong
46:  semi-regularity.  In red
47:  (resp. green), the average (resp. maximum) number of specializations
48:  for which the linear system is inconsistent. In blue, the bound for
49:  $\gamma$-strong regularity. Dashed line: absolute value of the first
50:  non positive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma
51:    n\rfloor,n}$. \label{fig:strongsemireg}}
52:\end{figure}
55:In Figure \ref{fig:strongsemireg}, we show the relation between the
56:value of the first nonpositive coefficient of the power series
57:expansion of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor \gamma n\rfloor,n}$ and
58:$\gamma$-strong semi-regularity for small values of $n=m$
59:(i.e. $\alpha=1$). For each $n$, the experiments are conducted on 1000
60:random quadratic boolean systems. For each of these systems, we
61:compute the $2^{\lceil (1-\gamma) n\rceil}$ specialized systems and we count
62:the number of specializations for which the filtering linear system is
63:inconsistent.
66:Four curves are represented on each chart in Figure
67:\ref{fig:strongsemireg}. The red (resp. green) one represents the
68:average (resp. maximal) number of specializations for which the linear
69:system (step 8 of Algorithm \textsf{BooleanSolve}) is
70:inconsistent. In contrast, the blue curve shows the upper bound on this
71:number of specializations required to be $\gamma$-strong semi-regular
72:(see Definition \ref{defn:strongsemireg}). The black curve shows the
73:absolute value of the first nonpositive coefficient of the
74:corresponding power series (i.e. $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma
75:  n\rfloor,n}$). The $y$-axis is represented in logarithmic scale. The
76:value $\gamma=0.1$ is never used in the complexity analysis (since in
77:Theorem \ref{thm:globalcompl}, $\gamma\geq .27$ for any value of
78:$\alpha\geq 1$). However, it is still interesting to study the
79:behavior of Algorithm \ref{algo:booleansolve} when almost all
80:variables are specialized: the filtering remains very efficient in
81:this case, and the branches which are explored during the second stage
82:of the exhaustive search correspond to those containing solutions of
83:the system. 
85:\medskip
87:\noindent \textbf{Interpretation of Figure \ref{fig:strongsemireg}.}
88:First, notice that for $\gamma\leq 0.55$ the green curve is always below
89:the blue one (except for the case $\gamma=.55, n=23$), meaning that
90:during our experiments, all randomly generated systems with those
91:parameters were $\gamma$-strong semi-regular.
93:Next, in most curves (except $\gamma=0.27$), the average (resp. maximal)
94:number of points where the specialization leads to an inconsistent
95:linear system is close to $1$ (resp. $5$). This can be explained by a
96:simple Poisson model.  Indeed, the
97:number of solutions of a random boolean system with as many equations
98:as unknowns follows a Poisson law with parameter $1$ (see~\cite{FusBac07}). Therefore, the
99:expectation of the number of solutions is $1$. The
100:expectation of the maximum of the number of solutions of $1000$ random
101:systems is then given as the maximum of 1000~iid random variables $P_1,\ldots, P_{1000}$ following a Poisson law of parameter $1$: 
102:\[\mathbf{E}(\max(P_1,\ldots, P_{1000}))=\sum_{k\ge1}{k\left((e^{-1}\sum_{i=0}^k{\frac{1}{i!}})^{1000}-(e^{-1}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}{\frac{1}{i!}})^{1000}\right)}\simeq 5.51,\]
103:which explains very well the observed behaviour.
105:This means that during Algorithm
106:\ref{algo:booleansolve} with these parameters, almost all
107:specializations giving rise to an inconsistent system correspond to a
108:branch of the exhaustive search which contains an actual solution of
109:the system. Therefore, the filtering is very efficient for those
110:parameters. 
112:\medskip
114:\noindent\textbf{Few specializations.} In the case $\gamma=0.9$, the blue curve has a negative slope. This is due to
115:the fact that the quantity $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)$ (see
116:Definition \ref{defn:strongsemireg}) is negative for $\alpha=1$ and
117:$\gamma>0.82308$. Therefore, we cannot expect that a large proportion
118:of boolean systems are $\gamma$-strong semi-regular in this setting. A
119:limit case is investigated in the chart corresponding to
120:$\gamma=0.81$. There, $1-2\gamma+2F_{\alpha}(\gamma)\approx 0.0102$ is
121:positive but very close to zero. Experiments show that random boolean
122:systems with these parameters and $10\leq n\leq 24$ are $\gamma$-strong
123:semi-regular with probability approximately equal to $0.75$.
125:\medskip
127:\noindent\textbf{Absolute value of the first nonpositive coefficient of
128:  $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma n\rfloor,n}$ and $\gamma$-strong
129:  semi-regularity.} Another interesting setting is $\gamma=.55,
130:n=23$. Here, no generated systems were $\gamma$-strong semi-regular
131:(although all generated systems for $n\neq 23$ were $\gamma$-strong
132:semi-regular). As explained in Section \ref{sec:proba}, this is due to
133:the fact that the first nonpositive coefficient of the power series
134:expansion of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma n\rfloor,n}$ is equal to
135:zero. In Section~\ref{sec:BoolDelta}, we show that this phenomenon
136:can be avoided by a simple variant of the algorithm.
140:A similar phenomenon happens for $\gamma=.27$: the first nonpositive
141:coefficient of the power series has small absolute value. It is an
142:accident due to the fact that this coefficient is close to zero for
143:$n\leq 25$ (see Figure \ref{fig:HScoeff}). On this chart, we can see
144:clearly the relation between the absolute value of the first
145:nonpositive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma n\rfloor,n}$
146:and the number of specializations for which the consistency test
147:fails.
149:Indeed, experiments on $1000$ random systems with $\gamma=.27$ and
150:$n=26$ were conducted and in this case the average number of
151:specializations for which the linear system is inconsistent is
152:$1$.
154:These experiments justify the fact that the complexity analysis
155:conducted in Section~\ref{sec:compl} is relevant for a large class of
156:boolean systems. Also, it shows that the random systems for which the
157:filtering may not be efficient can be detected \emph{a priori} by
158:looking at the absolute value of the first nonpositive coefficient in
159:the power series. If this value is small, we show in Section
160:\ref{sec:BoolDelta} that the quality of the filtering can be improved at low
161:cost by adding redundancy.
163:\begin{figure}
164:\centerline{
165:\fbox{\includegraphics[width=.5\textwidth]{HScoeff}}}
166:  \caption{Evolution of the logarithm of the absolute value of the first nonpositive coefficient of $\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma n\rfloor,n}$. \label{fig:HScoeff}}
167:\end{figure}
169:Figure \ref{fig:HScoeff} shows the evolution of the logarithm of the
170:absolute value of the first nonpositive coefficient of
171:$\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor\gamma n\rfloor,n}$. This absolute value seems to
172:grow exponentially with $n$ for any given $\gamma$.
173:Since the quality of the filtering is related to this absolute value, these experiments suggest that the proportion of $\gamma$-strong semi-regular systems tends towards 1 when $n$ grows, as formulated in Conjecture \ref{conj:propbeta}.
176:\subsection{Numerical estimates of the complexity}
177:When $n=m$ and in the most favorable algorithmic case, our complexity estimate
178:uses~$\gamma=.55$. For this value, we display in
179:Figure~\ref{fig:asympt} (page \pageref{fig:asympt}) a comparison of
180:the behaviour of~$\deg(\mathsf{HS}_{n,\lceil{\frac{n}{\gamma}}\rceil})/n$ and
181:its limit. 
182:This picture shows a relatively slow
183:convergence.  Thus, for a given number~$n$ of variables it is more
184:interesting to optimize~$\gamma$ using the exact value
185:of~$\deg(\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor{\gamma n}\rfloor,n})$ rather than a first order asymptotic estimate. In the same
186:spirit, one can also use the actual values given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:mac}
187:for the Macaulay matrix. Thus we seek to find~$\gamma$ that minimizes
188:the following bounds on the number of operations:
189:\begin{equation}\label{eq:nbops}
190:\begin{array}{l}
191:2^{(1-\gamma) n} r_{\mathsf{Mac}}c_{\mathsf{Mac}}\min(r_{\mathsf{Mac}},c_{\mathsf{Mac}})^{\theta-2}, \\\text{resp.}\quad 2^{(1-\gamma) n}\max(r_{\mathsf{Mac}},c_{\mathsf{Mac}})\log\max(r_{\mathsf{Mac}},c_{\mathsf{Mac}})s_{\mathsf{Mac}}
192:\end{array}\end{equation}
193:in the deterministic (resp. probabilistic) variants, using
194:Eq.~\eqref{eq:size_matrix} with~$n$ equations, $\lfloor{\gamma
195:  n}\rfloor$ variables and~$d=\deg(\mathsf{HS}_{\lfloor{\gamma
196:    n}\rfloor,n})$. The corresponding values of~$\gamma$ are given in
197:Figure~\ref{fig:beta}, together with the corresponding values of the
198:quantities in Eq.~\eqref{eq:nbops}. Although these values do not take
199:into account the constants hidden in the $O()$ estimates of the
200:complexity, they suggest the relevance of these algorithms in the
201:cryptographic sizes: the threshold between exhaustive search and our
202:algorithm with Gaussian elimination is~$n\simeq280$, while the
203:asymptotically faster Las Vegas variant starts being faster than
204:exhaustive search for~$n$ larger than~200 and beats deterministic
205:Gaussian elimination for $n$ larger than~160.
210:\begin{figure}
211:\centerline{\includegraphics[width=.4\textwidth]{fig_beta}
212:\hfil\includegraphics[width=.55\textwidth]{fig_compl}}
213:\caption{Left: optimal values of~$\gamma$ for the probabilistic 
214:variant (red), the deterministic variant with Gaussian
215: elimination (black) and Coppersmith-Winograd matrix
216:  multiplication (blue), and their limits. Right: 
217:  corresponding values of $\log_2N/n$, with $N$ given 
218:  by~\eqref{eq:nbops}. The green line corresponds to an 
219:  exhaustive search.\label{fig:beta}} 
220:\end{figure}

